   150	\Phi(u+v)=\Phi(u)+\Phi(v)+
   151	\sum_{t<m}\{f_t(u)+f_t(v)-f_t(u+v)\},
   152	\]
   153	so cross-scale incompatibility is real but cannot be charged to the shorter prefix: the prefix \(0^N,c_0\) has \(D=0\). Finite alphabets give a useful reduced dual using next/last exceedance times, and the one-level case has \(D_n=0\); for two levels there is an exact positive-part formula. No uncrossing/localization theorem is proved yet.
   154	
   155	Iteration 15 adds a local two-level damping identity. In the formal left-limit model, for
   156	\[
   157	h^M,0^N,\ell,h,\qquad 0<\ell<h,
   158	\]
   159	with \(n=M+N+2\),
   160	\[
   161	D_n=\bigl[\ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+\bigr]_+\left(1-\frac{\ell}{h}\right).
   162	\]
   163	Old high prices can therefore eliminate a later low/high conflict when the low-horizon comparator is already high-dominated. The stable \(h^-\) quote has exact regret
   164	\[
   165	[\ell(L_\ell(T)-1)_+-h(L_h(T)-1)_+]_+,
   166	\]
   167	but this quantity is only the regret of that stable policy, not an intrinsic conflict certificate. The path \(0^N,\ell,0^L,h\), \(L\gg N\), has linear stable-high regret at the low horizon while the low-only prefix has \(D=0\) and the full conflict is only \(N\ell(1-\ell/h)\). The two-level gluing target should use resolved-surplus/debt bookkeeping, not a raw \(Q_T\) dichotomy.

