   342	Since every fixed comparator earns at most \(\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}\),
   343	\[
   344	R_T\le \sum_{t<T}\left(M_{t,T}-g_\mu(M_{t,T})\right)\le (T-1)/e.
   345	\]
   346	Together with CL-027, this gives the sharp worst-case constant
   347	\[
   348	\sup_P \inf_{\pi\in\Pi_{\rm rand,ho}}
   349	\limsup_{T\to\infty} R_T^\pi(P)/T=1/e
   350	\]
   351	for deterministic exogenous paths, expected regret over learner randomization, `sup` comparators, and horizon-oblivious randomized policies. The upper bound actually uses a process-unaware policy and extends to exogenous stochastic paths by conditioning on the realized path. It fails as stated for endogenous prices that react to quotes; deterministic-policy existence is handled separately in CL-031.
   352	
   353	**CL-031: Nonconstructive deterministic derandomization of the \(1/e\) known-path upper bound.**  
   354	Status: accepted as a known-path/horizon-oblivious theorem, not as a tail characterization.  
   355	There exists a fixed deterministic sequence \(a_1,a_2,\ldots\in(e^{-1},1)\) and a finite constant \(C\) such that for every \(n\ge1\) and every nonincreasing sequence \(c_1\ge\cdots\ge c_n\) in \([0,1]\),
   356	\[
   357	\sum_{t=1}^n a_t1\{a_t<c_t\}
   358	\ge
   359	\sum_{t=1}^n (c_t-e^{-1})_+
   360	- C n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log(n+2)}.
   361	\]
   362	Proof: sample \(a_t\) iid from the equal-revenue law \(d\mu(a)=a^{-1}1\{e^{-1}\le a\le1\}\,da\), union-bound Hoeffding deviations over nonincreasing \(m_n\)-grid threshold sequences with \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\), round arbitrary thresholds downward, and use Borel-Cantelli to freeze one good realization.
   363	
   364	For any deterministic exogenous path, set \(M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s\). Since \(M_{1,T}\ge\cdots\ge M_{T-1,T}\), the fixed deterministic sequence satisfies
   365	\[
   366	R_T\le (T-1)/e + C T^{2/3}\sqrt{\log(T+2)}.
   367	\]
   368	Combined with CL-027, the sharp worst-case limsup constant for deterministic horizon-oblivious policies on deterministic exogenous paths is also \(1/e\). The result is nonconstructive, uses `sup` comparators, strict crossing, single-fill rewards, and exogeneity. It does not apply to endogenous/reactive prices and does not revive raw-tail necessity.
   369	
   370	**CL-032: Compact suffix-menu local value formula.**  
   371	Status: accepted as a local left-limit/infimum theorem; the explicit infinite dual law remains optional and needs separate measure-theoretic polishing.  
   372	Let \(C\subset[0,1]\) be compact, set \(C_+=C\cap(0,1]\), and if \(C_+=\emptyset\) define \(\delta(C)=0\). Otherwise let \(m=\max C_+\) and
   373	\[
   374	s_C(x)=\min(C_+\cap[x,m]),\qquad x\in(0,m].
   375	\]
   376	Define \(\delta(C)\in[0,m]\) as the unique root of the improper integral equation
   377	\[
   378	F_C(\delta):=\int_\delta^m \frac{dx}{s_C(x)}=1.
   379	\]
   380	Then \(\delta(C)\le m/e\), and in the formal left-limit action space
   381	\[
   382	\inf_{\nu\in\Delta([0,1])}\sup_{c\in C_+}
   383	\left[
   384	c-\int_{[0,c]}x\,d\nu(x)
   385	\right]
   386	=\delta(C).
   387	\]
   388	For actual strict-crossing quotes, this is an infimum statement: the upper certificate samples \(X\) on \([\delta,m]\) with density \(1/s_C(x)\,dx\) and posts \((1-\eta)s_C(X)\), losing at most \(O(\eta m)\) uniformly over \(c\in C\). The lower bound follows by finite submenus/Hausdorff approximations and CL-028/LB-023; arbitrary dense finite sets need only a.e. successor convergence, not pointwise convergence at every endpoint. This extends the finite-menu formula and singleton cases must be read with the same left-limit convention.
   389	
   390	**CL-033: Compact suffix-menu known-path upper theorem.**  
   391	Status: accepted as a sufficient theorem for deterministic known exogenous paths, not as a characterization.  
   392	For a deterministic exogenous path and horizon \(T\), let
   393	\[
   394	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad
   395	C_t=\overline{\{M_{t,T'}:T'>t\}}.
   396	\]
   397	Assume the learner is path-aware/horizon-oblivious and may choose at time \(t\) an actual quote distribution whose payoff against every \(c\in C_t\) is at least \(c-\delta(C_t)-\varepsilon_t\), where \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\). Then for every horizon,
   398	\[
   399	R_T\le \sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)+\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t.
   400	\]
   401	The proof uses \(r_t(a;T)=a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\), the local menu guarantee at \(c=M_{t,T}\), and the comparator upper bound
   402	\[
   403	\sup_a\sum_{t<T}a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\le\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}.
   404	\]
   405	Thus \(\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)=o(T)\) is sufficient for sublinear known-path anytime regret. The theorem is noncausal/path-oracle, requires exogeneity and `sup` comparators, and does not cover endogenous prices.
   406	
   407	**CL-035: Exact marginal reduction for deterministic known paths.**  
   408	Status: accepted under deterministic exogenous known-path, horizon-oblivious randomized-policy, expected pseudo-regret assumptions.  
   409	For a fixed deterministic exogenous path define
   410	\[
   411	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad
   412	V_T^*=\sup_{b\in[0,1]}\sum_{t<T}b1\{b<M_{t,T}\}.
   413	\]
   414	Then
   415	\[
   416	r_t(a;T)=a1\{a<M_{t,T}\},
   417	\]
   418	and for any horizon-oblivious randomized policy with marginal law \(\mu_t\) for \(A_t\),
   419	\[
   420	R_T
   421	=V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\int_{[0,M_{t,T})}x\,d\mu_t(x).
   422	\]
   423	Thus correlations among the learner's actions do not affect expected regret on a fixed deterministic path; only the one-time marginals matter. The same formalism gives a simple stable-comparator sufficient condition: if for some left-limit quote \(b\)
   424	\[
   425	V_T^*-b\,|\{t<T:M_{t,T}\ge b\}|=o(T),
   426	\]
   427	then posting \(b-\varepsilon_t\) with \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\) yields \(o(T)\) regret. This explains why the alternating path is easy despite linear suffix-menu cost. The reduction fails for endogenous/reactive prices and is not a causal online algorithm.
   428	
   429	**CL-036: Finite-prefix dual horizon-conflict modulus.**  
   430	Status: accepted as a finite-prefix left-limit/infimum theorem; sufficiency of its normalized asymptotics is open.  
   431	For a deterministic prefix \(P_{1:n}\), define the left-limit finite-prefix simultaneous value
   432	\[
   433	D_n(P)=
   434	\inf_{\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{n-1}}
   435	\max_{T\le n}
   436	\left[
   437	V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\int_{[0,M_{t,T}]}x\,d\mu_t(x)
   438	\right].
   439	\]
   440	Then finite LP duality gives
   441	\[
   442	D_n(P)=
   443	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta_n}
   444	\left[
   445	\sum_{T\le n}\lambda_TV_T^*
   446	-
   447	\sum_{t<n}\sup_{a\in[0,1]}
   448	\sum_{T>t}\lambda_T\,a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}
   449	\right],
   450	\]
   451	where the supremum is equivalently a maximum over formal left-limit thresholds
   452	\[
   453	C_t^n=\{M_{t,T}:T>t,\ M_{t,T}>0\}.
   454	\]
   455	In the original strict-crossing action space this is an infimum statement, implemented with quotes just below finitely many thresholds. If \(D_n(P)\ge\epsilon n\) infinitely often, every horizon-oblivious expected-regret policy on that path has \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge\epsilon\). The dual recovers singleton terminal blocks as easy, the \(0^N,1/2,1\) block as the basic \(1/4\) conflict, and equal-revenue blocks as the accepted \(1/e\)-type obstruction after the existing block accounting.
   456	
   457	**CL-037: Fixed-envelope compactness criterion for known-path anytime marginals.**  
   458	Status: accepted modulo standard compactness/Tychonoff facts, in the formal left-limit model; still tautological rather than structural.  
   459	Fix a deterministic exogenous path and a nonnegative envelope \(e_T\). There exists a formal horizon-oblivious randomized marginal sequence \((\mu_t)_{t\ge1}\) with
   460	\[
   461	V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\int_{[0,M_{t,T}]}x\,d\mu_t(x)\le e_T
   462	\qquad\forall T
   463	\]
   464	iff for every \(n\) there are finite-prefix marginals \(\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{n-1}\) satisfying the same inequalities for all \(T\le n\), with the same envelope \(e_T\) independent of \(n\). The proof uses compactness of \(\mathcal P([0,1])^\mathbb N\) and closedness of the constraints: \(x1\{x\le c\}\) is bounded upper semicontinuous, so the payoff is upper semicontinuous and the superlevel constraints are closed. If \(e_T=o(T)\), actual strict-crossing \(o(T)\) regret follows by replacing formal quote \(A_t\) with \((1-\eta_t)A_t\) and choosing \(\sum_{t<T}\eta_t=o(T)\). The weaker scalar condition \(D_n(P)=o(n)\) is necessary but not known sufficient.
   465	
   466	**CL-038: Last-exceedance structure and prefix-splicing robustness.**  
   467	Status: accepted as structural bookkeeping for deterministic known exogenous paths.  
   468	For a fixed quote \(b\), define
   469	\[
   470	L_b(T)=\max\{s\le T:P_s>b\},
   471	\]
   472	with \(L_b(T)=0\) if there is no such \(s\). Then
   473	\[
   474	M_{t,T}>b\iff t<L_b(T),
   475	\qquad
   476	\sum_{t<T}b1\{b<M_{t,T}\}=b(L_b(T)-1)_+.
   477	\]
   478	Thus the fixed-comparator value can be written as
   479	\[
   480	V_T^*=\sup_{b\in[0,1]} b(L_b(T)-1)_+,
   481	\]
   482	with the usual strict-crossing `sup`/left-limit convention. Also, if two marginal sequences \(\mu,\nu\) agree for all \(t>m\), then their horizon deficits
   483	\[
   484	d_T(\mu)=V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\bar g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})
   485	\]
   486	satisfy \(|d_T(\mu)-d_T(\nu)|\le m\) for every \(T>m\). This is only a crude upper bound on possible damage from prefix repair, not a lower bound saying such repair must cost \(m\).
   487	
   488	**CL-039: Increasing-threshold finite-prefix \(D_n\) stress test.**  
   489	Status: accepted as a finite-prefix bound in the formal left-limit model; exact value for general \(m\) remains open.  
   490	For the deterministic prefix
   491	\[
   492	0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m,\qquad 0<c_1<\cdots<c_m\le1,
   493	\]
   494	let \(C=\{c_1,\ldots,c_m\}\), \(n=N+m\), and define \(D_n\) as in CL-036. Then
   495	\[
   496	N\kappa(C)\le D_n(P)\le N\kappa(C)+m.
   497	\]
   498	The lower bound is the same cancellation used in the same-path multi-threshold lower bound: averaging the horizons \(T_j=N+j\) against a dual law for \(\kappa(C)\) leaves the zero-block deficit. The upper bound uses a local primal law for \(C\) on the first \(N\) quotes and pays at most \(m\) for all threshold-round slack. For \(m=2\), the intermediate threshold-round action can be chosen just below \(c_2\), so the left-limit value is exactly
   499	\[
   500	D_{N+2}=N\,\frac{c_1(c_2-c_1)}{c_2}.
   501	\]
   502	For \(m>2\), removing the additive \(m\)-scale slack or identifying the exact correction is still a useful finite LP problem.
   503	
   504	**CL-041: Exact separated two-threshold finite-prefix value and dual scale splitting.**  
   505	Status: accepted in the formal left-limit model, with strict-crossing implemented by \(a\mapsto(1-\eta)a\).  
   506	For the deterministic prefix
   507	\[
   508	0^N,c_0,0^L,c_1,\qquad 0<c_0<c_1\le1,
   509	\]
   510	with \(n=N+L+2\), the finite-prefix value from CL-036 is exactly
   511	\[
   512	D_n(P)=N\,\frac{c_0(c_1-c_0)}{c_1}.
   513	\]
   514	The lower bound restricts to the horizons \(T_0=N+1\) and \(T_1=n\); the post-\(c_0\) quote opportunities cancel at \(T_1\), leaving the first \(N\) zero-block two-threshold conflict. The upper bound uses the two-threshold local primal law on the first \(N\) quote times and posts \(c_1^-\) on all later quote times.
   515	
   516	For the finite-prefix dual functional
   517	\[
   518	\Phi(w)=\sum_Tw_TV_T^*
   519	-\sum_t f_t(w),\qquad
   520	f_t(w)=\sup_a a\sum_{T>t}w_T1\{a<M_{t,T}\},
   521	\]
   522	if \(w=u+v\), \(u\) is supported on \(T\le m\), and \(v\) on \(T>m\), then
   523	\[
   524	\Phi(u+v)=\Phi(u)+\Phi(v)+
   525	\sum_{t<m}\bigl(f_t(u)+f_t(v)-f_t(u+v)\bigr).
   526	\]
   527	Moreover the boundary term is bounded by
   528	\[
   529	\sum_{t<m}\min\left\{\sum_{T>t}u_T,\sum_Tv_T\right\}
   530	\le m\min(\|u\|_1,\|v\|_1).
   531	\]
   532	This identity shows the boundary term is a genuine cross-scale incompatibility, not a proof artifact.
   533	
   534	**CL-042: Finite-alphabet left-limit dual reduction.**  
   535	Status: accepted as an exact finite-prefix formula; no localization theorem follows yet.  
   536	For a deterministic prefix whose positive prices lie in
   537	\[
   538	0<c_1<\cdots<c_k,
   539	\]
   540	work in the formal left-limit model where action \(c_j^-\) earns \(c_j\) when the suffix maximum is at least \(c_j\). Let
   541	\[
   542	N_j(t)=\min\{s>t:P_s\ge c_j\},
   543	\quad
   544	L_j(T)=\max\{s\le T:P_s\ge c_j\},
   545	\]
   546	with \(N_j(t)=\infty\) and \(L_j(T)=0\) when the corresponding event is absent, and write
   547	\[
   548	\Lambda_s=\sum_{T=s}^n\lambda_T,\qquad \Lambda_\infty=0.
   549	\]
   550	Then the finite-prefix dual in CL-036 becomes
   551	\[
   552	D_n(P)=\max_{\lambda\in\Delta_n}
   553	\left[
   554	\sum_{T\le n}\lambda_T
   555	\max_j c_j(L_j(T)-1)_+
   556	-
   557	\sum_{t<n}\max_j c_j\Lambda_{N_j(t)}
   558	\right].
   559	\]
   560	The one-positive-level case has \(D_n(P)=0\) for every prefix. For two levels \(\ell<h\), set
   561	\[
   562	A_T=(L_\ell(T)-1)_+,\quad B_T=(L_h(T)-1)_+,
   563	\]
   564	\[
   565	U_t=\lambda\{T:L_\ell(T)>t\},\quad
   566	W_t=\lambda\{T:L_h(T)>t\}.
   567	\]
   568	For any dual law \(\lambda\),
   569	\[
   570	\Phi(\lambda)
   571	=
   572	\mathbb E_\lambda[(\ell A_T-hB_T)_+]
   573	-
   574	\sum_{t<n}(\ell U_t-hW_t)_+.
   575	\]
   576	This is the cleanest finite-alphabet test bed for the open \(D_n=o(n)\) gluing problem, but the desired uncrossing/localization theorem is still unproved.
   577	
   578	**CL-044: Two-level single-drought damping identity.**  
   579	Status: accepted as a local finite-prefix identity in the formal left-limit model.  
   580	For \(0<\ell<h\), consider the deterministic prefix
   581	\[
   582	h^M,0^N,\ell,h
   583	\]
   584	of length \(n=M+N+2\). Then
   585	\[
   586	D_n(P)=
   587	\bigl[\ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+\bigr]_+
   588	\left(1-\frac{\ell}{h}\right).
   589	\]
   590	Here \(D_n\) is the finite-prefix value from CL-036 in the left-limit model, with `sup` comparators and single-fill rewards. The proof uses only the two active horizons \(T_0=M+N+1\) and \(T_1=n\). Let
   591	\[
   592	H=(M-1)_+,\qquad G=N+\mathbf 1_{\{M\ge1\}},
   593	\qquad S=[\ell(M+N)-hH]_+.
   594	\]
   595	The primal quotes \(h^-\) off the \(G\) drought coordinates and places total \(\ell^-\)-mass \(S/h\) on those coordinates; the low-horizon and final-high deficits both become \(S(1-\ell/h)\). The matching dual puts weights \(1-\ell/h\) and \(\ell/h\) on \(T_0,T_1\) and uses
   596	\[
   597	(1-\ell/h)a1\{a\le\ell\}+(\ell/h)a1\{a\le h\}\le\ell
   598	\]
   599	in the formal threshold compactification. Strict crossing in the original action space requires \(\eta\)-implementation. The identity is local and does not imply additive decomposition across many droughts.
   600	
   601	**CL-045: Stable-high exact regret on two-level paths.**  
   602	Status: accepted as a simple consequence of the last-exceedance representation.  
   603	On a deterministic path whose positive levels are \(0<\ell<h\), define \(L_\ell(T)\) and \(L_h(T)\) as the last times by \(T\) at which the path reaches the corresponding level, with value \(0\) if absent. In the formal left-limit model,
   604	\[
   605	V_T^*=\max\{\ell(L_\ell(T)-1)_+,\,h(L_h(T)-1)_+\}.
   606	\]
   607	The policy that always posts \(h^-\) earns \(h(L_h(T)-1)_+\), hence has exact regret
   608	\[
   609	Q_T=[\ell(L_\ell(T)-1)_+-h(L_h(T)-1)_+]_+.
   610	\]
   611	Thus if highs recur and \(Q_T=o(T)\), stable \(h^-\) gives \(o(T)\) regret; if highs eventually stop, an eventual stable \(\ell^-\) policy is enough. This is an upper-bound observation about stable policies, not a lower-bound certificate.
   612	
   613	## Refuted Or Weakened
   614	
   615	**CL-006: Original iff conjecture.**  
   616	Status: refuted under literal instance-wise reading; unresolved under class-uniform/minimax readings.  
   617	Reason: CL-005.
   618	
   619	**CL-007: \(\gamma(h)\to0\) as an instance-wise necessary condition.**  
   620	Status: refuted for process-aware/instance-wise learnability.  
   621	Reason: coded-delay construction can have \(\gamma(h)\ge1/4\) for all \(h\), yet the process makes \(a=1/2\) always optimal and an always-\(1/2\) learner has zero regret.
   622	
   623	**CL-008: \(\Delta_T(H_T)=o(T)\) as an instance-wise necessary condition.**  
   624	Status: refuted or at least strongly weakened.  
   625	Reason: predictable sparse spike examples have linear horizon-tail loss along infinitely many horizons while a horizon/process-aware learner can quote the known near-optimal level and get \(o(T)\) regret.
   626	
   627	**CL-009: \(\Delta_T\) is the weakest visible truncation gap.**  
   628	Status: false as stated.  
   629	A smaller comparator-level quantity is
   630	\[
   631	\Gamma_T(H)=\sup_a V_T(a)-\sup_a V_T^H(a),
   632	\]
   633	with \(\Gamma_T(H)\le\Delta_T(H)\). Even \(\Gamma_T\)-type raw reward tails may not be necessary if delayed mass is predictable.
   634	
   635	**CL-010: Comparator maxima always exist.**  
   636	Status: false.  
   637	With strict crossing \(P_s>a\), atoms can make the best fixed quote unattained; e.g. deterministic future price \(c\) gives payoff approaching \(c\) as \(a\uparrow c\) but not at \(a=c\). Use \(\sup\) or add assumptions ensuring attainment.
   638	
   639	**CL-034: Suffix-menu smallness is not necessary.**  
   640	Status: refuted as a necessary condition for known-path anytime learnability.  
   641	On the deterministic alternating path
   642	\[
   643	P_{2k-1}=1/2,\qquad P_{2k}=1,
   644	\]
   645	the suffix menus are \(C_t=\{1\}\) for odd \(t\) and \(C_t=\{1/2,1\}\) for even \(t\), so
   646	\[
   647	\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)\sim T/8.
   648	\]
   649	Nevertheless the horizon-oblivious path-aware policy posting \(1-\varepsilon_t\), with average \(\frac1T\sum_{t\le T}\varepsilon_t\to0\), has \(o(T)\) regret for all horizons: it nearly matches the comparator \(1^-\), losing only the accumulated \(\varepsilon_t\)'s plus \(O(1)\) near horizons ending at a \(1/2\). Hence CL-033 is only a sufficient condition; the local bound can be loose because it compares the fixed comparator to \(\sum_tM_{t,T}\), not to the exact \(\sup_a\sum_t a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\).
   650	
   651	**CL-043: Boundary defect does not imply a shorter-prefix \(D_m\) certificate.**  
   652	Status: refuted as a localization route.  
   653	For the separated block in CL-041, split at \(m=N+1\) and choose two-threshold dual weights on \(T_0=N+1\) and \(T_1=N+L+2\). The boundary defect on the first \(N\) quote coordinates equals
   654	\[
   655	N\,\frac{c_0(c_1-c_0)}{c_1},
   656	\]
   657	while the shorter prefix \(0^N,c_0\) has \(D_m=0\) in the left-limit model. Thus a large support-function additivity defect may come from late shadow thresholds invisible inside the prefix. Any successful localization proof must either keep a boundary-augmented certificate or prove that repeated cross-scale defects force large \(D_n\) at some comparable scale.
   658	
   659	**CL-046: The raw stable-high gap is not a two-level localization certificate.**  
   660	Status: refuted as a dichotomy route.  
   661	For a two-level path define
   662	\[
   663	Q_T=[\ell(L_\ell(T)-1)_+-h(L_h(T)-1)_+]_+.
   664	\]
   665	By CL-045 this is exactly the regret of the stable \(h^-\) quote, but \(Q_T=\Omega(T)\) at a low horizon does not force a comparable-scale finite-prefix conflict. The finite stress test
   666	\[
   667	0^N,\ell,0^L,h,\qquad L\gg N,
   668	\]
   669	has \(Q_{N+1}=\ell N\), while the low-only prefix \(0^N,\ell\) has \(D_{N+1}=0\) by quoting \(\ell^-\), and the full prefix has
   670	\[
   671	D_{N+L+2}=N\ell(1-\ell/h)
   672	\]
   673	by CL-041, which is \(o(N+L)\) if \(L\gg N\). Thus a successful two-level gluing proof must track when low-surplus is resolved by a later high and how low-quote debt accumulates; \(Q_T/T\) alone is too coarse.
   674	
   675	## Candidate Claims Needing More Work
   676	
   677	**CL-011: Envelope-strengthened sparse spikes.**  
   678	Status: plausible with finite-prefix and monotonicity conditions.  
   679	For eventually positive nondecreasing \(q(T)=o(T)\), one can choose spike amplitudes \(c_n\) after the eventual threshold so regret is at most \(q(T)/2\) for all sufficiently large \(T\), while \(\beta\equiv1\). Needs a clean statement with \(c_n>0\), \(c_n/s_n\le1\), and finite-prefix handling.
   680	
   681	**CL-012: Rare-history essential-sup obstruction.**  
   682	Status: plausible but not yet proved.  
   683	Rare positive-probability branches may keep \(\beta\) or \(\gamma\) large via essential sup while contributing negligible expected regret. Need a fully specified process and horizon calculations.
   684	
   685	**CL-013: Information-theoretic necessity condition.**  
   686	Status: promising direction.  
   687	A lower bound should use two processes/classes with identical observations before delayed feedback resolves, but different best fixed quotes. Long tail mass must be payoff-relevant, sufficiently frequent, and unpredictable.
   688	
   689	**CL-020: Prefix-compatibility modulus.**  
   690	Status: promising candidate abstraction, not yet a global theorem/characterization.  
   691	For a common prefix \(I\) and candidate continuations or horizons \(u\in\mathcal U\), define conditional expected prefix payoffs
   692	\[
   693	\phi_{u,t}(a)=\mathbb E_u[a\,1\{\exists s\in(t,T_u]:P_s>a\}\mid\text{common prefix}]
   694	\]
   695	and \(v_u=\sup_b\sum_{t\in I}\phi_{u,t}(b)\). A local simultaneous-payoff gap is
   696	\[
   697	\kappa_I(\mathcal U)=\frac1{|I|}\inf_{\mu_t}\sup_{u\in\mathcal U}
   698	\left[v_u-\sum_{t\in I}\int \phi_{u,t}(a)\,d\mu_t(a)\right].
   699	\]
   700	For homogeneous zero-block deterministic thresholds \(\{c_0,c_1\}\), the finite-grid/limit calculation gives \(\kappa=\alpha(c_0,c_1)\). To transfer this gap to regret, one must add: same prefix action law across candidates, candidates interpreted as conditional expected payoff laws rather than realized hidden futures, a cap \(B_u\) on outside/post-divergence learner surplus, and probability weighting for rare prefix events. Small \(\kappa\) is not by itself a learnability theorem.
   701	
   702	**CL-030: Path-dependent suffix-menu upper framework.**  
   703	Status: finite version accepted and compact-menu cleanup promoted to CL-032/CL-033; necessity and computable selectors remain open.  
   704	For a deterministic exogenous path, define each quote time's future suffix-max menu
   705	\[
   706	C_t=\{M_{t,T'}:T'>t\}.
   707	\]
   708	If a horizon-oblivious learner chooses \(\mu_t\) with
   709	\[
   710	\int_{[0,c)}a\,d\mu_t(a)\ge c-\delta_t
   711	\qquad\text{for all }c\in C_t,
   712	\]
   713	then for every horizon
   714	\[
   715	R_T\le\sum_{t<T}\delta_t.
   716	\]
   717	For finite \(C_t\), CL-028 identifies the optimal local \(\delta_t\) as \(\delta_*(C_t)=\kappa(C_t)\). For compact menus, CL-032/CL-033 provide the approximation cleanup and strict-crossing \(\eta\)-implementation route. Noncompact menus, computable selectors, and necessity remain open. This is an upper framework for known deterministic paths, not a necessity theorem for raw delay tails.
   718	
   719	**CL-040: Market-specific gluing from \(D_n=o(n)\).**  
   720	Status: open conjectural route, not proved.  
   721	The desired theorem would say that for deterministic known exogenous paths, the scalar condition \(D_n(P)=o(n)\), together with market-specific structure of the suffix maxima, implies a projectively consistent horizon-oblivious marginal sequence with \(o(T)\) regret. Iteration 13 narrowed the plausible route to dual localization: if no global fixed sublinear envelope exists, prove that some finite-prefix dual certificate can be supported on comparable-scale horizons, forcing \(D_n(P)=\Omega(n)\). The generic compactness argument does not prove this, and an abstract non-market parity example shows scalar finite-prefix smallness alone is insufficient in arbitrary compact feasibility problems. Any proof must exploit more than mere monotonicity of \(M_{t,T}\), which every exogenous path already has; possible stronger handles are last-exceedance laminarity, finite price alphabets, record-level stability, or scale-sensitive dual constraints.
   722	
   723	Iteration 14 weakens the naive dual-localization route. The exact boundary identity in CL-041 shows cross-scale conflict can live entirely in early quote coordinates without producing a shorter-prefix \(D_m\) certificate, and CL-043 refutes the direct "boundary defect implies local conflict" inequality. The finite-alphabet reduction CL-042 is now the best positive test bed; a two-level localization theorem remains open, and even alternating two-level prefixes require care because exact \(D_n=0\) is not true for all ratios \(\ell/h\), only in stable-comparator/easy cases up to bounded effects.
   724	
   725	Iteration 15 adds a sharper two-level local regression test, CL-044, and refutes the proposed \(Q_T\)-based dichotomy via CL-046. The revised positive target is a resolved-surplus or debt recursion: low-horizon surplus \(S\) should matter only relative to the prefix length at the high that resolves it, and low-level quote mass creates persistent high debt that must be controlled cumulatively across droughts. This remains unproved, especially with multiple lows in one drought or overlapping drought intervals.

