    57	## LB-006: Sparse Vanishing-Spike Counterexample
    58	
    59	Let \(s_n=2^n\), \(P_{s_n}=U_n/s_n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise. Then at \(t=s_n\), \(a=0\) has \(\tau_{s_n}(0)=s_{n+1}-s_n=2^n\) almost surely, so \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\). For fixed \(a>0\), if \(m^*=\max\{m:s_m\le T,\ a<1/s_m\}\) exists, only \(t<s_{m^*}\) can earn and \(\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\le a s_{m^*}<1\); if it does not exist, reward is zero. Thus \(R_T\le1\) for the always-zero learner. Status: accepted.
