   123	## LB-011: Same-Path Two-Horizon Incompatibility
   124	
   125	Consider the deterministic path
   126	\[
   127	P_1=\cdots=P_N=0,\qquad P_{N+1}=1/2,\qquad P_{N+2}=1.
   128	\]
   129	For any horizon-oblivious policy, the first \(N\) actions are the same whether regret is evaluated at \(T_0=N+1\) or \(T_1=N+2\). Define
   130	\[
   131	G_0=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^N A_t1\{A_t<1/2\},
   132	\qquad
   133	G_1=\mathbb E\sum_{t=1}^N A_t1\{A_t<1\}.
   134	\]
   135	For every action \(a\),
   136	\[
   137	a1\{a<1/2\}+a1\{a<1\}\le1,
   138	\]
   139	so \(G_0+G_1\le N\). Using \(\eta\)-comparators, the two regrets obey
   140	\[
   141	R_{N+1}+R_{N+2}\ge N(1/2-2\eta)-O(1),
   142	\]
   143	where the \(O(1)\) term covers endpoint actions such as the quote posted at time \(N+1\). Hence one of the two prefix regrets is at least \(N(1/4-\eta)-O(1)\). Status: accepted finite lemma for horizon-oblivious policies; the dominant-block concatenation is now recorded as LB-013.
   144	
   145	## LB-012: Concatenated Universal Block Lower Bound
   146	
   147	Fix a randomized process-unaware learner, or a process-unaware horizon-aware family \((\pi_T)_T\). Choose block lengths with
   148	\[
   149	M_i=\sum_{j<i}(N_j+1)=o(N_i).
   150	\]
   151	After blocks \(1,\ldots,i-1\) are fixed, compare the two continuations
   152	\[
   153	\text{prefix},0^{N_i},1/2
   154	\qquad\text{and}\qquad
   155	\text{prefix},0^{N_i},1
   156	\]
   157	at the same endpoint \(T_i=M_i+N_i+1\), using the same learner randomness and the same horizon-indexed policy if applicable. During the \(N_i\) zero rounds the histories coincide. If
