   232	## LB-015: Prefix-Compatibility LP Candidate
   233	
   234	For a finite action grid and finite candidate set \(\mathcal U\), define a common-prefix simultaneous-payoff gap
   235	\[
   236	\kappa_I(\mathcal U)=\frac1{|I|}\inf_{\mu_t}\sup_{u\in\mathcal U}
   237	\left[
   238	v_u-\sum_{t\in I}\int \phi_{u,t}(a)\,d\mu_t(a)
   239	\right],
   240	\]
   241	where \(\phi_{u,t}(a)\) is the conditional expected payoff of posting \(a\) at prefix time \(t\) under candidate \(u\), and
   242	\[
   243	v_u=\sup_b\sum_{t\in I}\phi_{u,t}(b).
   244	\]
   245	On homogeneous zero blocks, \(\phi_{u,t}=\phi_u\), the dual candidate is
   246	\[
   247	\kappa(\mathcal U)=
   248	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta(\mathcal U)}
   249	\left[
   250	\sum_u\lambda_u v_u-\sup_a\sum_u\lambda_u\phi_u(a)
   251	\right],
   252	\]
   253	first on finite grids, then by \(\eta\)-comparator/limit arguments for strict thresholds.
   254	
   255	For \(\mathcal U=\{c_0,c_1\}\) with \(\phi_c(a)=a1\{a<c\}\), the dual yields
   256	\[
   257	\kappa(\{c_0,c_1\})=\frac{c_0(c_1-c_0)}{c_1}.
   258	\]
   259	
   260	Status: promising but not accepted as a global characterization. A regret-transfer lemma needs the same prefix action law across candidates and an outside-surplus cap \(B_u\):
   261	\[
   262	R_{T_u}\ge |I|\kappa_I(\mathcal U)-B_u
   263	\]
   264	for at least one candidate, under appropriate indistinguishability. Rare prefix events must be weighted by their probability in expected regret.
   265	
   266	## LB-016: Conditional Kappa Transfer
   267	
   268	Let \(\mathcal U\) be finite, \(I\) a tested prefix, and \(E_u\) a common-prefix event under candidate \(u\) with probability \(p_u\). Define
   269	\[
   270	\phi_{u,t}(a)=\mathbb E_u[r_t(a)\mid E_u],
   271	\qquad
   272	v_u=\sup_b\sum_{t\in I}\phi_{u,t}(b),
   273	\]
   274	over the same action domain being lower-bounded. Suppose the learner has common conditional prefix action laws \(\mu_t\) across all \(u\), and the stronger payoff factorization
   275	\[
   276	\mathbb E_u[1_{E_u}r_t(A_t)]
   277	=p_u\int\phi_{u,t}(a)\,d\mu_t(a).
   278	\]
   279	Suppose also that \(V_u^*\ge p_uv_u+C_u\) and the learner's expected reward outside the tested prefix-on-\(E_u\) is at most \(C_u+B_u\). Then
   280	\[
   281	R_u\ge p_u\left[v_u-\sum_{t\in I}\int\phi_{u,t}(a)\,d\mu_t(a)\right]-B_u.
   282	\]
   283	If \(p_u\ge p\), \(B_u\le B\), and
   284	\[
   285	\kappa_I(\mathcal U)=\frac1{|I|}\inf_{\mu_t}\sup_u
   286	\left[v_u-\sum_{t\in I}\int\phi_{u,t}(a)\,d\mu_t(a)\right]\ge0,
   287	\]
   288	then
   289	\[
   290	\max_u R_u\ge p\,|I|\kappa_I(\mathcal U)-B.
   291	\]
   292	Status: accepted as a conditional bookkeeping lemma. Same action marginals alone are insufficient unless the payoff factorization is true. The action domain must match the learner or be handled by a dense-grid/\(\eta\)-comparator limit.
   293	
   294	## LB-017: Multi-Threshold Zero-Block LP Duality
   295	
   296	For a zero block followed by a terminal threshold \(c\in C\subset(0,1]\), the local continuum prefix gap is
   297	\[
   298	\kappa(C)=
   299	\inf_{\mu\in\Delta([0,1])}\sup_{c\in C}
   300	\left[c-\int a1\{a<c\}\,d\mu(a)\right].
   301	\]
   302	For a finite action grid \(A\), finite LP duality gives
   303	\[
   304	\kappa_A(C)=
   305	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta(C)}
   306	\left[\mathbb E_\lambda C-\max_{a\in A}a\,\Pr_\lambda(C>a)\right].
   307	\]
   308	Taking dense-grid/left-limit suprema gives the continuum formula
   309	\[
   310	\kappa(C)=
   311	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta(C)}
   312	\left[\mathbb E_\lambda C-\sup_{a\in[0,1]}a\,\Pr_\lambda(C>a)\right].
   313	\]
   314	For any law \(C\in[0,1]\), with \(R=\sup_a a\Pr(C>a)\),
   315	\[
   316	\mathbb E C=\int_0^1\Pr(C>x)\,dx
   317	\le R+R\log(1/R),
   318	\]
   319	and therefore
   320	\[
   321	\mathbb E C-R\le R\log(1/R)\le 1/e.
   322	\]
   323	When \(R=0\), this is interpreted as the trivial \(C=0\) almost surely case.
   324	The bound is approached by the truncated equal-revenue tail
   325	\[
   326	\Pr(C>x)=1\quad (x<1/e),\qquad
   327	\Pr(C>x)=1/(ex)\quad (1/e\le x<1),
   328	\]
   329	with an atom at \(1\). Status: accepted as local LP/minimax geometry only. The dual distribution is not itself a hard stochastic process under \(\sup_a\mathbb E-\mathbb E\) pseudo-regret.
   330	
   331	## LB-018: Finite-\(m\) Equal-Revenue Theorem
   332	
   333	For \(m\ge1\), let \(Z\in[0,1]\) have at most \(m\) positive support points and define
   334	\[
   335	\rho(Z)=\sup_{a\in[0,1]}a\Pr(Z>a).
   336	\]
   337	If \(k\le m\) is the number of positive support points, then
   338	\[
   339	\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)\le \left(\frac{k-1}{k}\right)^k
   340	\le \left(\frac{m-1}{m}\right)^m,
   341	\]
   342	where the \(k=1\) value is \(0\). The bound for \(m\) is attained for \(m\ge2\) by the discrete equal-revenue law
   343	\[
   344	r=\frac{m-1}{m},\qquad c_i=r^{m-i},\qquad \Pr(Z\ge c_i)=r^{i-1},\qquad i=1,\ldots,m.
   345	\]
   346	For this law the left-limit posted-price revenue is \(r^{m-1}\) throughout the relevant support intervals and
   347	\[
   348	\mathbb EZ-r^{m-1}=r^m=\left(\frac{m-1}{m}\right)^m.
   349	\]
   350	
   351	Proof skeleton: zero atoms are harmless because conditioning on \(Z>0\) scales both \(\mathbb EZ\) and \(\rho(Z)\). Scaling by the top positive support scales the gap. After these reductions, replacing \(Z\) by \(\max\{Z,\rho(Z)\}\) increases \(\mathbb EZ\), preserves the positive support count, and does not increase \(\rho\). Thus write the support as
   352	\[
   353	\rho=c_1<c_2<\cdots<c_k=1.
   354	\]
   355	With \(p_i=\Pr(Z\ge c_i)\), strict crossing gives \(c_ip_i\le\rho\) as a left-limit revenue bound. Hence
   356	\[
   357	\mathbb EZ
   358	=c_1+\sum_{i=2}^k(c_i-c_{i-1})p_i
   359	\le
   360	\rho+\rho\sum_{i=2}^k\left(1-\frac{c_{i-1}}{c_i}\right).
   361	\]
   362	Set \(y_i=c_{i-1}/c_i\). Since \(\prod_{i=2}^k y_i=\rho\), AM-GM gives \(\sum y_i\ge (k-1)\rho^{1/(k-1)}\). Maximizing
   363	\[
   364	\rho(k-1)\left(1-\rho^{1/(k-1)}\right)
   365	\]
   366	over \(\rho\in[0,1]\) gives the stated constant. Status: accepted local posted-price geometry; not a stochastic hard instance under pseudo-regret.
   367	
   368	## LB-019: Finite Multi-Continuation Terminal Block
   369	
   370	Let \(C\subset(0,1]\) be finite and consider deterministic terminal-block instances \(0^N,c\), \(c\in C\). Suppose the learner's first \(N\) action law is common across all continuations. Define
   371	\[
   372	\kappa(C)=
   373	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta(C)}
   374	\left[
   375	\mathbb E_\lambda C-\sup_{a\in[0,1]}a\Pr_\lambda(C>a)
   376	\right].
   377	\]
   378	For each fixed continuation \(c\), the zero-block comparator value is \(Nc\) as a supremum. If \(\mu_t\) is the common law of the learner's \(t\)-th zero-block quote, then averaging regret with any \(\lambda\in\Delta(C)\) gives
   379	\[
   380	\sum_c\lambda_c R_c
   381	\ge
   382	N\mathbb E_\lambda C
   383	-
   384	\sum_{t=1}^N
   385	\int a\Pr_\lambda(C>a)\,d\mu_t(a)
   386	\ge
   387	N\kappa(C).
   388	\]
   389	Therefore \(\max_{c\in C}R_c\ge N\kappa(C)\). After a fixed old prefix of length \(M\), the same proof gives \(N\kappa(C)-M\) by capping all old learner rewards by \(M\).
   390	
   391	Specializing to the \(m\)-point equal-revenue set in LB-018 yields the constant \(((m-1)/m)^m\). In a universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction, recursively selecting a worst continuation gives
   392	\[
   393	R_{T_i}\ge N_i\left(\frac{m-1}{m}\right)^m-M_i.
   394	\]
   395	If \(m=m_i\to\infty\) and \(M_i/N_i\to0\), the lower-bound constant approaches \(1/e\). Status: accepted for universal/process-unaware terminal-block lower bounds; use `sup`/left-limit or \(\eta\)-comparator conventions for strict crossing.
   396	
   397	## LB-020: Same-Path Multi-Threshold Anytime Lift
   398	
   399	Let an arbitrary deterministic old prefix have length \(M\), then append
   400	\[
   401	0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m,\qquad 0<c_1<\cdots<c_m\le1.
   402	\]
   403	Set \(T_j=M+N+j\). Consider one horizon-oblivious policy, possibly process-aware and randomized, whose zero-block actions \(A_1,\ldots,A_N\) are the same random variables for all evaluations \(T_j\). Regret is against comparator suprema, and each quote pays at most once.
   404	
   405	For each \(j\),
   406	\[
   407	R_{T_j}\ge
   408	N c_j-\mathbb E\sum_{s=1}^N A_s1\{A_s<c_j\}-M.
   409	\]
   410	Indeed, a fixed quote \(a\uparrow c_j\) earns supremum \((N+j-1)c_j\) from the \(N\) zero-block posts and the \(j-1\) earlier threshold-round posts. The learner's reward by \(T_j\) is at most \(M\) from the old prefix, plus
   411	\[
   412	\mathbb E\sum_{s=1}^N A_s1\{A_s<c_j\}
   413	\]
   414	from the current zero block, plus \((j-1)c_j\) from the earlier threshold-round posts. The latter term cancels the comparator's extra threshold-round value.
   415	
   416	For any \(\lambda\in\Delta(C)\),
   417	\[
   418	\sum_j\lambda_j R_{T_j}
   419	\ge
   420	N\mathbb E_\lambda C
   421	-
   422	\sum_{s=1}^N
   423	\mathbb E[A_s\Pr_\lambda(C>A_s)]
   424	-M
   425	\ge
   426	N\left(\mathbb E_\lambda C-\sup_a a\Pr_\lambda(C>a)\right)-M.
   427	\]
   428	Optimizing over \(\lambda\) gives
   429	\[
   430	\max_j R_{T_j}\ge N\kappa(C)-M.
   431	\]
   432	Status: accepted for same-path horizon-oblivious lower bounds. The increasing order of thresholds is essential; otherwise the effective threshold by \(T_j\) is \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), not \(c_j\). Horizon-aware policies, comparator `max`, or repeated-fill rewards invalidate the stated proof.
   433	
   434	## LB-021: Same-Path Equal-Revenue Diagonal Corollary
   435	
   436	Let \(m_i\to\infty\), \(r_i=(m_i-1)/m_i\), and
   437	\[
   438	c_{i,j}=r_i^{m_i-j},\qquad j=1,\ldots,m_i.
   439	\]
   440	Build one deterministic path by concatenating blocks \(0^{N_i},c_{i,1},\ldots,c_{i,m_i}\). With
   441	\[
   442	M_i=\sum_{\ell<i}(N_\ell+m_\ell),
   443	\]
   444	choose \(N_i\) so that \((M_i+m_i)/N_i\to0\). Applying LB-020 and LB-018 to block \(i\) gives some \(j_i\) with
   445	\[
   446	R_{M_i+N_i+j_i}
   447	\ge
   448	N_i r_i^{m_i}-M_i.
   449	\]
   450	Since \(M_i+N_i+j_i\le M_i+N_i+m_i\) and \(r_i^{m_i}\to1/e\),
   451	\[
   452	\limsup_T R_T/T\ge1/e.
   453	\]
   454	Status: accepted for process-aware but horizon-oblivious expected-regret policies on this deterministic known path. This is the lower-bound half of the sharp randomized worst-case \(1/e\) theorem; LB-024 supplies the matching upper bound. It is not a full path-dependent characterization.
   455	
   456	## LB-022: Equal-Revenue Primal Zero-Block Certificate
   457	
   458	For the \(m\)-point equal-revenue menu in LB-018, the local zero-block game is tight from the primal side. Let \(r=(m-1)/m\), \(c_i=r^{m-i}\), and use the action law that is uniform over quotes \(a_i\uparrow c_i\). For threshold \(c_j\),
   459	\[
   460	\frac1m\sum_{i\le j}c_i
   461	=c_j-r^m,
   462	\]
   463	so the zero-block deficit is \(r^m=((m-1)/m)^m\) for every \(j\), matching the dual value. Status: accepted as a finite equal-revenue local certificate with the usual left-limit convention; LB-023 generalizes the primal optimizer to arbitrary finite \(C\).
   464	
   465	## LB-023: Explicit Finite-Menu Local Formula
   466	
   467	Let \(C=\{0<c_1<\cdots<c_m\}\), set \(c_0=0\), and define
   468	\[
   469	F_C(\delta)=\sum_{i=1}^m
   470	\frac{(c_i-\delta)_+-(c_{i-1}-\delta)_+}{c_i}.
   471	\]
   472	Then \(F_C\) has a unique root \(\delta_*=\delta_*(C)\in[0,c_m]\) with \(F_C(\delta_*)=1\). The local zero-block value is
   473	\[
   474	\kappa(C)=
   475	\inf_{\mu\in\Delta([0,1])}\sup_{c\in C}
   476	\left[c-\int a1\{a<c\}\,d\mu(a)\right]
   477	=\delta_*,
   478	\]
   479	with values understood as left limits or as infima over \(\eta\)-quotes below thresholds.
   480	
   481	Upper bound: put formal mass
   482	\[
   483	q_i=\frac{(c_i-\delta_*)_+-(c_{i-1}-\delta_*)_+}{c_i}
   484	\]
   485	on \(c_i^-\). Since \(\sum_iq_i=F_C(\delta_*)=1\), this is a probability law in the left-limit compactification. For threshold \(c_j\),
   486	\[
   487	\sum_{i\le j}q_i c_i=(c_j-\delta_*)_+,
   488	\]
   489	so the deficit is \(\min\{c_j,\delta_*\}\le\delta_*\). Actual actions \(c_i-\eta_i\) give the same bound up to \(o(1)\).
   490	
   491	Lower bound: let \(k=\min\{i:c_i>\delta_*\}\). On the active suffix define
   492	\[
   493	\Pr(Z\ge c_i)=c_k/c_i,\qquad i\ge k.
   494	\]
   495	Then the left-limit posted-price revenue is \(\rho(Z)=c_k\), and tail summation plus \(F_C(\delta_*)=1\) gives \(\mathbb EZ=c_k+\delta_*\). The LP dual formula LB-017 gives \(\kappa(C)\ge\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=\delta_*\).
   496	
   497	Boundary checks: for \(m=1\), \(\delta_*=0\) and the value is an infimum; for \(C=\{c,d\}\), \(\delta_*=c(d-c)/d\); if \(\delta_*=c_r\), thresholds \(c_j\le\delta_*\) are nonbinding and the active suffix begins at the first \(c_i>\delta_*\). Status: accepted local geometry.
   498	
   499	## LB-024: Suffix-Max Representation And Universal Equal-Revenue Upper Bound
   500	
   501	For a deterministic exogenous path and horizon \(T\), define
   502	\[
   503	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s.
   504	\]
   505	Under the single-fill reward convention,
   506	\[
   507	r_t(a;T)=a1\{M_{t,T}>a\}.
   508	\]
   509	For a quote distribution \(\mu_t\), write
   510	\[
   511	g_{\mu_t}(c)=\int_{[0,c)}a\,d\mu_t(a).
   512	\]
   513	The learner's expected reward from quote \(t\) by horizon \(T\) is \(g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})\).
   514	
   515	The universal equal-revenue distribution
   516	\[
   517	d\mu(a)=a^{-1}1\{e^{-1}\le a\le1\}\,da
   518	\]
   519	has
   520	\[
   521	g_\mu(c)=0\quad(c\le e^{-1}),\qquad
   522	g_\mu(c)=c-e^{-1}\quad(c>e^{-1}).
   523	\]
   524	Therefore \(c-g_\mu(c)\le e^{-1}\) for all \(c\in[0,1]\). Since
   525	\[
   526	\sup_a\sum_{t<T}a1\{M_{t,T}>a\}\le\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T},
   527	\]
   528	the policy satisfies
   529	\[
   530	R_T\le\sum_{t<T}\left(M_{t,T}-g_\mu(M_{t,T})\right)\le (T-1)/e.
   531	\]
   532	Status: accepted for exogenous deterministic paths and randomized expected regret. By conditioning on the realized path, the same upper bound applies to exogenous stochastic paths. It is not valid for price processes that react to the realized quote.
   533	
   534	## LB-025: Local Suffix-Menu Upper Bound
   535	
   536	For a deterministic exogenous path, let
   537	\[
   538	C_t=\{M_{t,T'}:T'>t\}.
   539	\]
   540	If the learner chooses \(\mu_t\) satisfying
   541	\[
   542	g_{\mu_t}(c)\ge c-\delta_t
   543	\quad\text{for every }c\in C_t,
   544	\]
   545	then for every horizon
   546	\[
   547	R_T\le\sum_{t<T}\delta_t.
   548	\]
   549	Indeed,
   550	\[
   551	\mathbb E\sum_{t<T}r_t(A_t;T)
   552	=\sum_{t<T}g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})
   553	\ge\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}-\sum_{t<T}\delta_t,
   554	\]
   555	while the fixed comparator supremum is at most \(\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}\). For finite menus, the best \(\delta_t\) is \(\delta_*(C_t)\) from LB-023. Compact menus are handled by LB-028/LB-029 with left-limit/\(\eta\)-implementation conventions. Status: accepted as a conditional upper lemma; noncompact menus and computable selectors remain open.
   556	
   557	## LB-026: Monotone-Threshold Deterministic Derandomization
   558	
   559	Let \(\mu\) be the equal-revenue law
   560	\[
   561	d\mu(a)=a^{-1}1\{e^{-1}\le a\le1\}\,da,
   562	\qquad
   563	g(c)=\mathbb E_\mu[A1\{A<c\}]=(c-e^{-1})_+.
   564	\]
   565	There exists a deterministic sequence \(a_1,a_2,\ldots\in(e^{-1},1)\) and a finite constant \(C\) such that for every \(n\) and every nonincreasing \(c_1\ge\cdots\ge c_n\in[0,1]\),
   566	\[
   567	\sum_{t=1}^n a_t1\{a_t<c_t\}
   568	\ge
   569	\sum_{t=1}^n g(c_t)-C n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log(n+2)}.
   570	\]
   571	
   572	Proof skeleton. Draw \(A_t\stackrel{iid}{\sim}\mu\). For fixed \(n\), set \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\) and let \(\mathcal D_{n,m}\) be all nonincreasing sequences in the grid \(\{0,1/m,\ldots,1\}\). Then
   573	\[
   574	|\mathcal D_{n,m}|\le {n+m\choose m}.
   575	\]
   576	For fixed \(d\in\mathcal D_{n,m}\), the variables
   577	\[
   578	X_t(d)=A_t1\{A_t<d_t\}
   579	\]
   580	are independent and lie in \([0,1]\), with mean \(g(d_t)\). Hoeffding gives
   581	\[
   582	\Pr\left\{
   583	\sum_t X_t(d)<\sum_t g(d_t)-u
   584	\right\}\le \exp(-2u^2/n).
   585	\]
   586	Choose
   587	\[
   588	u_n=\sqrt{\frac n2\left(\log|\mathcal D_{n,m_n}|+4\log(n+2)\right)}.
   589	\]
   590	A union bound gives failure probability at most \((n+2)^{-4}\), hence summable. By Borel-Cantelli, with positive probability all but finitely many grid events hold. Freeze one realization satisfying them and absorb the finitely many exceptional \(n\)'s into \(C\).
   591	
   592	For arbitrary nonincreasing \(c_t\), set \(d_t=\lfloor m_nc_t\rfloor/m_n\). Then \(d_t\le c_t\), \(c_t-d_t\le1/m_n\),
   593	\[
   594	a_t1\{a_t<c_t\}\ge a_t1\{a_t<d_t\},
   595	\qquad
   596	g(d_t)\ge g(c_t)-1/m_n.
   597	\]
   598	Thus the total loss is \(u_n+n/m_n=O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\).
   599	
   600	Status: accepted as a nonconstructive probabilistic-method lemma. A bracketing-entropy proof may improve the displayed rate to \(O(n^{2/3})\), but that sharper rate is not needed and should be treated as optional until written.
   601	
   602	## LB-027: Deterministic \(1/e\) Known-Path Upper Bound
   603	
   604	Let \(a_t\) be a deterministic sequence from LB-026. For any deterministic exogenous path and horizon \(T\), define
   605	\[
   606	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad t<T.
   607	\]
   608	Under the single-fill reward convention,
   609	\[
   610	r_t(a_t;T)=a_t1\{a_t<M_{t,T}\}.
   611	\]
   612	The sequence \(M_{1,T}\ge\cdots\ge M_{T-1,T}\) is nonincreasing, so LB-026 gives
   613	\[
   614	\sum_{t<T}r_t(a_t;T)
   615	\ge
   616	\sum_{t<T}g(M_{t,T})-C T^{2/3}\sqrt{\log(T+2)}.
   617	\]
   618	For any fixed comparator \(a\),
   619	\[
   620	\sum_{t<T}a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\le \sum_{t<T}M_{t,T},
   621	\]
   622	and for every \(c\in[0,1]\), \(c-g(c)\le e^{-1}\). Therefore
   623	\[
   624	R_T\le (T-1)/e+C T^{2/3}\sqrt{\log(T+2)}.
   625	\]
   626	Status: accepted for deterministic exogenous paths, horizon-oblivious deterministic policies, `sup` comparators, strict crossing, and single-fill rewards. By conditioning on the realized path, the same pathwise upper bound yields the corresponding exogenous stochastic pseudo-regret bound. It is not valid for endogenous/reactive prices.
   627	
   628	## LB-028: Compact Suffix-Menu Local Formula
   629	
   630	Let \(C\subset[0,1]\) be compact. If \(C_+=C\cap(0,1]\) is empty, set \(\delta(C)=0\). Otherwise let \(m=\max C_+\) and
   631	\[
   632	s_C(x)=\min(C_+\cap[x,m]),\qquad x\in(0,m].
   633	\]
   634	There is a unique \(\delta=\delta(C)\in[0,m]\) with
   635	\[
   636	\int_\delta^m \frac{dx}{s_C(x)}=1,
   637	\]
   638	where the integral is improper at \(0\) when needed. Moreover \(\delta\le m/e\).
   639	
   640	In the formal left-limit action space,
   641	\[
   642	\inf_{\nu\in\Delta([0,1])}\sup_{c\in C_+}
   643	\left(c-\int_{[0,c]}x\,d\nu(x)\right)=\delta(C).
   644	\]
   645	
   646	Upper certificate: draw \(X\) on \([\delta,m]\) with density \(1/s_C(x)\,dx\), and post the formal quote \(s_C(X)^-\). For \(c\in C_+\),
   647	\[
   648	\mathbb E[s_C(X)1\{s_C(X)\le c\}]=(c-\delta)_+,
   649	\]
   650	so the deficit is at most \(\delta\). Actual strict-crossing quotes \((1-\eta)s_C(X)\) implement the same guarantee up to \(O(\eta m)\).
   651	
   652	Lower proof route: for finite \(F\subset C_+\), LB-023 gives the finite value \(\delta(F)\), and the compact value is at least \(\sup_F\delta(F)\). Finite Hausdorff approximations \(F_n\subset C_+\) with \(m\in F_n\) give \(s_{F_n}\to s_C\) a.e. on active intervals, so \(\delta(F_n)\to\delta(C)\) by convergence of the integral roots. Boundary cases with \(\delta(C)=0\) are handled by nonnegativity. Status: accepted; the direct infinite dual survival law is useful intuition but not needed for the proof.
   653	
   654	## LB-029: Compact Suffix-Menu Known-Path Upper Bound
   655	
   656	For a deterministic exogenous path, define
   657	\[
   658	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad
   659	C_t=\overline{\{M_{t,T'}:T'>t\}}.
   660	\]
   661	Suppose a path-aware, horizon-oblivious policy chooses at time \(t\) a quote distribution such that for all \(c\in C_t\),
   662	\[
   663	\mathbb E[A_t1\{A_t<c\}]\ge c-\delta(C_t)-\varepsilon_t,
   664	\]
   665	with \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\). Then for every horizon \(T\),
   666	\[
   667	R_T\le \sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)+\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t.
   668	\]
   669	
   670	Proof: under exogeneity and single-fill rewards,
   671	\[
   672	r_t(a;T)=a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}.
   673	\]
   674	Summing the local guarantee at \(c=M_{t,T}\) gives learner reward at least
   675	\[
   676	\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}-\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)-\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t.
   677	\]
   678	For every fixed comparator \(a\),
   679	\[
   680	\sum_{t<T}a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\le\sum_{t<T}M_{t,T}.
   681	\]
   682	Taking the comparator supremum proves the bound. Status: accepted as a sufficient known-path theorem only; it is noncausal/path-oracle and can be loose as a characterization.
   683	
   684	## LB-030: Exact Marginal Reduction For Deterministic Known Paths
   685	
   686	For a deterministic exogenous path and horizon \(T\), set
   687	\[
   688	M_{t,T}=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s.
   689	\]
   690	Then under the single-fill convention
   691	\[
   692	r_t(a;T)=a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}.
   693	\]
   694	If a horizon-oblivious randomized policy has marginal law \(\mu_t\) for \(A_t\), then
   695	\[
   696	\mathbb E\sum_{t<T}r_t(A_t;T)
   697	=
   698	\sum_{t<T}\int_{[0,M_{t,T})}x\,d\mu_t(x).
   699	\]
   700	Consequently, with
   701	\[
   702	V_T^*=\sup_{b\in[0,1]}\sum_{t<T}b1\{b<M_{t,T}\},
   703	\]
   704	the exact expected regret is
   705	\[
   706	R_T=V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\int_{[0,M_{t,T})}x\,d\mu_t(x).
   707	\]
   708	Correlations and adaptation to previous deterministic-path fills do not affect expected pseudo-regret beyond these one-time marginals. Status: accepted for deterministic exogenous known paths; not valid for endogenous/reactive prices.
   709	
   710	Stable-comparator corollary: if there is a left-limit quote \(b\in(0,1]\) with
   711	\[
   712	V_T^*-b|\{t<T:M_{t,T}\ge b\}|=o(T),
   713	\]
   714	then actual quotes \(b-\varepsilon_t\), \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\), give \(o(T)\) regret. This explains the alternating path \(1/2,1,1/2,1,\ldots\), where \(b=1\) is asymptotically optimal.
   715	
   716	## LB-031: Finite-Prefix LP Dual
   717	
   718	For a deterministic prefix \(P_{1:n}\), define the formal left-limit payoff
   719	\[
   720	\bar g_{\mu_t}(c)=\int_{[0,c]}x\,d\mu_t(x)
   721	\]
   722	and
   723	\[
   724	D_n(P)=
   725	\inf_{\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{n-1}}
   726	\max_{T\le n}
   727	\left[
   728	V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\bar g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})
   729	\right].
   730	\]
   731	Then
   732	\[
   733	D_n(P)=
   734	\max_{\lambda\in\Delta_n}
   735	\left[
   736	\sum_{T\le n}\lambda_TV_T^*
   737	-
   738	\sum_{t=1}^{n-1}
   739	\sup_{a\in[0,1]}\sum_{T>t}\lambda_Ta1\{a<M_{t,T}\}
   740	\right].
   741	\]
   742	For each \(t\), the per-time supremum equals the maximum over formal quotes just below
   743	\[
   744	C_t^n=\{M_{t,T}:T>t,\ M_{t,T}>0\},
   745	\]
   746	because between adjacent thresholds the indicator pattern is fixed and the objective is increasing in \(a\). In the original strict-crossing action space, the same value is an infimum, approximated by quotes \(c-\eta\).
   747	
   748	Proof: reduce each time \(t\) to the finite left-limit menu \(C_t^n\cup\{0\}\). The primal LP minimizes \(z\) subject to
   749	\[
   750	z\ge V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\sum_aq_{t,a}\phi_{t,T}(a),\qquad
   751	\sum_aq_{t,a}=1.
   752	\]
   753	Dualizing gives horizon weights \(\lambda_T\ge0\), \(\sum_T\lambda_T=1\), and independent per-time maximization terms, yielding the displayed formula. Status: accepted finite-prefix theorem.
   754	
   755	Certificate consequence: any \(\lambda\in\Delta_n\) gives a lower bound on \(\max_{T\le n}R_T\). In particular, if \(D_n(P)\ge\epsilon n\) for infinitely many \(n\), then every horizon-oblivious expected-regret policy on that deterministic path has \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge\epsilon\). The converse direction \(D_n=o(n)\Rightarrow\) existence of one \(o(T)\)-regret policy is open because finite-prefix optimizers may be projectively inconsistent.
   756	
   757	## LB-032: Fixed-Envelope Compactness Criterion
   758	
   759	Fix a deterministic exogenous path and a sequence \(e_T\ge0\). In the formal left-limit model, there exists a horizon-oblivious randomized marginal sequence \((\mu_t)_{t\ge1}\) such that
   760	\[
   761	V_T^*-\sum_{t<T}\bar g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})\le e_T
   762	\qquad\forall T
   763	\]
   764	iff for every \(n\) there exist \(\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_{n-1}\) satisfying these inequalities for all \(T\le n\), with the same envelope \(e_T\) independent of \(n\).
   765	
   766	Proof sketch: \(\mathcal P([0,1])\) is weakly compact, hence so is the countable product. For fixed \(T\), \(x1\{x\le c\}\) is bounded upper semicontinuous, so \(\mu\mapsto\bar g_\mu(c)\) is upper semicontinuous. Therefore the constraint
   767	\[
   768	\sum_{t<T}\bar g_{\mu_t}(M_{t,T})\ge V_T^*-e_T
   769	\]
   770	is closed. The finite-prefix feasibility assumptions give the finite intersection property, hence a global point. Necessity is immediate by restriction.
   771	
   772	Strict-crossing implementation: if the formal policy satisfies envelope \(e_T\), posting \((1-\eta_t)A_t\) with \(A_t\sim\mu_t\) loses at most \(\eta_t\) per quote. Thus
   773	\[
   774	R_T^{\rm actual}\le e_T+\sum_{t<T}\eta_t.
   775	\]
   776	Choosing \(\sum_{t<T}\eta_t=o(T)\) converts any formal \(e_T=o(T)\) policy into an actual strict-crossing \(o(T)\)-regret policy. Status: accepted modulo standard compactness facts; exact but tautological.
   777	
   778	## LB-033: Last-Exceedance Comparator Representation
   779	
