    66	## CE-008: Two-Continuation Block Lower-Bound Gadget And Universal Concatenation
    67	
    68	Status: accepted finite-block lower bound; universal/process-unaware concatenation accepted in iteration 4.
    69	
    70	Let \(P_1=\cdots=P_N=0\) and \(P_{N+1}=c\), where \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\). Before the terminal price, the learner sees identical histories in the two instances. Any randomized learner must split between quotes below \(1/2\), which work on both continuations but under-earn when \(c=1\), and quotes above \(1/2\), which fail when \(c=1/2\). With regret against suprema,
    71	\[
    72	\sup_{c\in\{1/2,1\}}R^\pi(I_c^N)\ge N/4.
    73	\]
    74	
    75	Iteration 4 verifies the dominant-block concatenation. For any process-unaware randomized learner, or process-unaware horizon-aware family, choose \(N_i\) with previous length \(M_i=o(N_i)\) and recursively pick the worse continuation \(c_i\). At endpoint \(T_i=M_i+N_i+1\),
    76	\[
    77	R_{T_i}\ge N_i/4-M_i,
    78	\]
    79	so \(\limsup_i R_{T_i}/T_i\ge1/4\). The proof caps all rewards from earlier quotes by \(M_i\), since each old quote pays at most once and at most \(1\).
