     1	# Literature Map
     2	
     3	## Full-Information Delayed Online Learning
     4	
     5	- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (ICML/PMLR 2013) is the primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction. Verified source states the ICML 2013/PMLR venue and frames delay effects on regret; use this for the finite-grid delayed-learning step, after explicitly mapping public price observations to delayed full-information reward vectors.
     6	- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (AAAI 2016), "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization," is verified. The source states a black-box reduction from delayed-feedback to standard online learning for full-information OCO and adaptive-gradient algorithms. Useful for horizon-free/adaptive variants of the grid reduction.
     7	- Quanrud and Khashabi (NeurIPS/NIPS 2015) gives adversarial-delay regret in terms of total delay \(D\), with source page reporting \(O(\sqrt D)\)-type control. This supports bounded-delay scaling via \(D\lesssim TH\), but still needs model mapping.
     8	- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
     9	- Van der Hoeven and Cesa-Bianchi (AISTATS/PMLR 2022) treats arm-dependent delays in nonstochastic bandits and experts. Verified source says the full-information experts setting has first-order bounds depending on delays/losses of the best arm. Relevant because quote delays are arm-dependent.
    10	
    11	## Unrestricted, Skipping, And Aggregate Delay Bounds
    12	
    13	- Thune, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin (NeurIPS 2019) handles nonstochastic bandits with unbounded delays and skipping excessively delayed observations; verified source gives bounds involving total delay and skip/oracle quantities. Useful for replacing a hard \(H\) truncation by adaptive skipping.
    14	- Zimmert and Seldin (AISTATS 2020/arXiv:1910.06054) gives optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined excluded-round sets. Verified through arXiv; relevance is conceptual for skip-set tail penalties.
    15	- Lancewicki, Segal, Koren, and Mansour (ICML/PMLR 2021) studies stochastic bandits with unrestricted and infinite delays, with quantile-based delay dependence. Verified source; relevant for quantile alternatives to uniform tails.
    16	- Zierahn, van der Hoeven, Lancewicki, Rosenberg, and Cesa-Bianchi (JMLR 2025) gives a modern unified analysis for delayed nonstochastic bandit feedback. Verified source; useful terminology but less direct because the truncated market-making reduction is full-information.
