   407	# Iteration 7 Consolidation
   408	
   409	## Accepted Progress
   410	
   411	- Promoted the finite-\(m\) equal-revenue theorem. If \(Z\in[0,1]\) has at most \(m\) positive support points and \(\rho(Z)=\sup_a a\Pr(Z>a)\), then
   412	\[
   413	\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)\le \left(\frac{m-1}{m}\right)^m.
   414	\]
   415	The sharp example is the discrete equal-revenue law \(c_i=r^{m-i}\), \(\Pr(Z\ge c_i)=r^{i-1}\), \(r=(m-1)/m\). Strict crossing requires `sup`/left-limit revenue language.
   416	- Accepted the finite multi-continuation terminal-block lower bound for universal/process-unaware quantifiers:
   417	\[
   418	\max_{c\in C}R_c\ge N\kappa(C)-M.
   419	\]
   420	For equal-revenue \(m\)-point menus this gives \(((m-1)/m)^m\), and diagonalizing \(m_i\to\infty\) gives a universal terminal-block constant approaching \(1/e\).
   421	
   422	## Candidate Routes
   423	
   424	- Write the finite-\(m\) theorem as a polished proposition with base cases, zero-atom conditioning, scaling, low-value collapse, and AM-GM.
   425	- Prove the same-path multi-threshold anytime lift on \(0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m\), with explicit intermediate-threshold reward bookkeeping.
   426	- Derive the primal optimizer for the finite equal-revenue zero-block game and test whether it informs a known-path simultaneous-prefix framework.
   427	- Build a rare-event multi-threshold stress test to show why local/essential-sup hardness must be probability-weighted.
   428	
   429	## Refuted Or Weakened Claims
   430	
   431	- The finite-\(m\) dual law is still not a hard stochastic process under \(\sup_a\mathbb E-\mathbb E\) pseudo-regret.
   432	- The constant \(((m-1)/m)^m\) is not the gap of every fixed \(m\)-threshold set; e.g. \(\{0.9,1\}\) has two-threshold gap \(0.09\).
   433	- Any lift from local \(\kappa\) to regret still requires same-prefix action laws and outside-surplus caps.
