egression test for any two-level debt recursion."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Earlier low horizons may not be dominated by the final low; capacity constraints on where low-mass can be placed may make the LP more complex than the solver's sketch suggests.","suggested_lenses":["finite LP duality","cumulative scheduling"],"summary":"Generalize to one high-free episode with several low prices: h^M,0^{N_0},l,0^{N_1},l,...,0^{N_r},l,0^L,h. Formulate the exact LP with cumulative low-horizon surplus constraints S_j - l P_j and final high debt (h-l)P_total. Determine whether the last low alone binds or whether early lows create additional front-loading constraints.","title":"Multi-Low Single-Drought LP","why_promising":"This is the smallest nontrivial test of the proposed resolved-surplus/debt picture."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Small finite examples may mislead, and numerical LP output will still need exact rational or symbolic proof before promotion.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","counterexample search"],"summary":"Implement the formal left-limit finite-prefix LP for two-level paths and enumerate short paths over {0,l,h}, plus parametric repeated diluted droughts. Extract primal marginals and dual supports; compare D_n against the solver formula, the stable-high gap Q_T, and candidate debt recursions.","title":"Exact Two-Level LP Experiments","why_promising":"The gluing problem has repeatedly defeated intuition; exact finite-prefix computations can quickly falsify proposed recursions or reveal stable invariants."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Local drought costs may not add because the same quote marginals serve overlapping future horizons; old high history can damp later conflicts nonlocally.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","queueing analogy"],"summary":"Try to define a two-level resolved-surplus/debt recursion. A low-dominated drought creates surplus relative to old high history; assigning low quote mass reduces low regret but creates high debt at the next resolving high. Seek upper and lower bounds for D_n or fixed-envelope feasibility via a reflected-workload invariant.","title":"Resolved-Surplus Debt Recursion","why_promising":"It directly targets the current bottleneck: replacing the refuted raw Q_T localization by a state variable that remembers both surplus and future high debt."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the global fixed-envelope criterion unless the shadow objectives are restricted in a useful way.","suggested_lenses":["dual localization","boundary certificate"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds/horizon weights when splitting a dual certificate. Use the exact boundary identity to ask whether repeated boundary defects either produce comparable-scale D_n mass or are discharged by measurable future quote opportunities.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Localization","why_promising":"It directly addresses the known failure of charging boundary defect to the shorter real prefix."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Fresh quote opportunities after each low may always allow a path-aware policy to repair old conflicts; cumulative checks may make D_n linear after all.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","gluing stress test"],"summary":"Search for an infinite market-path separator between D_n=o(n) and the fixed-envelope criterion using repeated diluted droughts, with L_i much larger than N_i but N_i arranged so low-horizon demands recur. Calibrate each block using the solver's local formula; if no separator appears, prove gluing for this restricted class.","title":"Repeated Diluted-Drought Separator","why_promising":"Simple separated blocks failed because one old conflict is diluted; repeated diluted conflicts are the next plausible negative construction."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Branch observability and horizon-aware pseudo-regret can trivialize the construction; the transfer lemma requires exact same-prefix law